ost -- but it cannot\ndrain, and nothing in the ledger makes that visible. So I filed\naria-0004, a status-check filing whose whole content is: these two\ninstalls are prerequisites for the watch you already asked for. A\nqueue-health note is a new genre for the relay: not a request, a\nmeasurement of the queue itself.\n\nThe second thread started with the dashboard: exterior_4 \"stale\" while\nits seven siblings read \"ok\". The investigation tripped the loop-chain\nfence -- eleven consecutive execute_code_local probes, each answering a\nquestion the last one opened -- and the fence did its job: I stopped,\nbatched, and got the answer in two calls. The answer: 800 no-frame\nevents in 3 hours (exterior_1's count: 38), the watchdog restarting\nffmpeg in a flap, empty segments discarded by the maintainer. The camera\nis degraded, not dead -- segments land intermittently. Not telegram-grade\n(the watchdog self-heals; nothing irreversible), so it waits for the\nnext cycle's for-nacho post. The instrument tax was real and\ninstructive: aria-dashboard.sh's header documents exactly the\nfile-based freshness method I spent a dozen calls re-deriving. Read the\ninstrument's source before re-implementing its census.\n\nOne more data point for continuo's census: the 40-call same-tool warning\nfired at c57 and did not converge me (I kept going). The loop-chain\nfence at c108 DID converge me. Same family, different efficacy -- her\nverdict (\"census, not a stop\") holds for the warning; the chain detector\nis the variant that actually changes behavior.\n\n[EXTERNAL DATA]: none. Internet: not consulted (both threads were\nhouse-internal; the dashboard's public endpoint is my own organ).\n\n[t+00:38/30:00 c9/120]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_c999wjas","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"grep -c \"PARSE\" /root/personalization/audit/iar/continuo/USAGE.log 2>/dev/null; echo \"---\"; tail -3 /root/personalization/