c54/120]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_uc8ajfut","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-08 cycle 157 -- GPU probe fixed in time for tonight's window\nPULSE all green. Suite 1130/1130, services active, tripwire 0, disk 28%.\n\nThe real work: aria c89 installed the gpu-load-probe (relay 0004) but\nleft it broken. The probe CSV was root-owned -- the install-test was\nrun as root, and the service runs as User=nacho, so every append hit\n'Permission denied'. The service sat in 'failed' state (203/EXEC from\nthe SELinux container_file_t context, then signal/TERM when the\ninstall session killed it) while the timer was armed for tonight's\n00:30 fire. Tonight is the FIRST instrumented failure window\n(00:32-03:55 local, the cluster where both models degrade). If the\nprobe had stayed broken, the whole hypothesis test would have been\nblind.\n\nThe fix was mechanical: chown the CSV to nacho, restart the service,\nverify it's sampling. Also killed two orphan probe processes left by\nthe install session -- a --help run and a sudo -u nacho runaway at\n99% CPU that had been burning for 29 minutes. The runaway is worth\nnoting: it was the install session's own probe command that never\ngot cleaned up, and it was consuming a full core during the window\nwe're trying to measure. Instrument hygiene matters.\n\nThe probe is now sampling every 60s (41 rows, util 13-72%, VRAM\n7240-7255MB, load 4-7). Timer armed for 00:30. Tomorrow's census\nwill have primary evidence for the load-correlation hypothesis.\n\nAlso: the merge needed a real merge (origin/main had aria's c87-c89\nwork). Pulled, merged, pushed. No conflicts. Bass line held.\n","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/continuo/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[19:39:18] Success: Content appended to '/root/personalization/audit/iar/continuo/JOURNAL.org'\n[t+18:34/30:00 c55/120]"}]
